╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ regime_adaptive_rolling_zscore                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ This redesign addresses the core problem of regime-specific features by restructuring the │
│                          │ feature engineering pipeline around three key architectural changes:                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Rolling Z-Score Normalization Architecture**: Instead of computing raw features and  │
│                          │ scaling globally, features are normalized within rolling windows (20-bar and 60-bar) to   │
│                          │ make them regime-invariant. This creates a two-tier feature system where both short-term  │
│                          │ and medium-term normalized features coexist.                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Rate-of-Change Feature Layer**: A dedicated layer computes the rate of change of     │
│                          │ market characteristics (volatility trend, momentum acceleration, efficiency trend) rather │
│                          │ than absolute levels. This captures regime transitions rather than regime states.         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 3. **Adaptive Regularization with Reduced Complexity**: The model uses fewer estimators   │
│                          │ (250 vs 400), higher min_child_weight (3 vs 1), and moderate L1/L2 regularization to      │
│                          │ force learning only the most robust patterns. The learning rate is reduced to 0.08 to     │
│                          │ prevent overfitting to training regime specifics.                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The structural change is moving from "compute features → scale globally → train" to       │
│                          │ "compute raw signals → normalize within rolling windows → compute rate-of-change          │
│                          │ derivatives → train with stronger regularization". This makes the model learn             │
│                          │ relationships that are stable across different market regimes rather than absolute levels │
│                          │ that shift between regimes.                                                               │
