│ patch_name               │ short_regime_invariant_features                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ This edit attacks the main failure mode directly: the model’s slope collapses             │
│                          │ out-of-sample because many current features are absolute-level regime dependent and use   │
│                          │ long lookbacks that decay across regimes.                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I’m proposing two coordinated changes:                                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. Feature engineering:                                                                   │
│                          │    - Shift to shorter windows (mostly 3–24 bars) so signals adapt faster.                 │
│                          │    - Add rolling z-scored versions of returns and volume so the model sees                │
│                          │ regime-normalized inputs instead of raw magnitude.                                        │
│                          │    - Add short-horizon trend/efficiency/volatility-change features to capture the *rate   │
│                          │ of change* in market character.                                                           │
│                          │    - Keep a small number of robust interaction terms, especially momentum × volatility    │
│                          │ regime.                                                                                   │
│                          │    - Remove some long lookback / redundant features to stay well under the 80-feature     │
│                          │ limit.                                                                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. XGBoost regularization:                                                                │
│                          │    - Make the model slightly more conservative in tree depth but more robust via higher   │
│                          │ min_child_weight and moderate regularization.                                             │
│                          │    - Reduce n_estimators and learning rate a bit to limit training-set specialization     │
│                          │ while keeping enough capacity for short-horizon nonlinearities.                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This is designed to improve val slope and pred_std_ratio without pushing train-val gap    │
│                          │ too wide.                                                                                 │
